    "description": "Kurdish culture in Iran includes varied linguistic, literary, architectural, and clothing traditions. Regional differences, including the distinctive Hawraman landscape, make a single uniform portrait misleading.",
    "tags": ["Kurdish culture", "Hawraman", "Kurdish literature", "Regional dress", "Zagros"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:09:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:09:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Kurdish Culture in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover regional differences in Kurdish language, literature, dress, and the living cultural landscape of Hawraman."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "language-and-belonging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Language and belonging",
        "content": "Kurdish culture in Iran is best approached as a connected set of regional traditions. Linguistic scholarship distinguishes northern, central, and southern Kurdish varieties, with different distributions across Iran and neighboring countries. Central Kurdish is commonly called Sorani; southern varieties are associated particularly with the Kermanshah region. These distinctions matter when choosing a book, recording, or translation: “Kurdish” alone may not identify the language variety a reader will encounter. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/iran-vi2-documentation/?generate_pdf=1)\n\nCultural belonging and linguistic classification do not always coincide neatly. Philip Kreyenbroek’s account of Kurdish literature discusses Gurani as a language with a different linguistic history that nevertheless became important within Kurdish literary culture. Its place in that history illustrates why a community’s cultural repertoire cannot be reduced to a single linguistic family tree. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/kurdish-written-literature/?generate_pdf=1)",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "literary-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Literature across languages and forms",
        "content": "The Ardalan ruling family, associated with the region around present-day Kurdistan Province, supported Gurani literary production. Kreyenbroek situates this patronage within a wider history in which Kurdish writers also participated in Arabic, Persian, and Turkish literary worlds. Written Kurdish poetry drew on established regional forms, while oral narratives and poetry remained important. Prose literature expanded during the twentieth century. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/kurdish-written-literature/?generate_pdf=1)\n\nThese connections invite readers to look beyond an opposition between “folk” and “literary” culture. A written work can use forms shared with neighboring traditions while expressing a distinct language and cultural setting. Equally, the prominence of oral transmission should not be mistaken for an absence of literary sophistication. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/kurdish-written-literature/?generate_pdf=1)",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hawraman-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hawraman: culture built into a landscape",
        "content": "Hawraman, also rendered Uramanat, offers a particularly tangible example of regional specificity. UNESCO’s account locates its cultural landscape in the Zagros Mountains across Kurdistan and Kermanshah provinces. Terraced gardens, settlements built on steep slopes, seasonal dwellings, and movements between elevations connect architecture with farming and livestock keeping. Transhumance means this seasonal movement of people and animals between grazing areas. [s3](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1647/)\n\nThis landscape is useful for understanding how practical knowledge becomes cultural heritage: maintaining a terrace or coordinating seasonal work connects a household to a larger inhabited environment. It should not, however, stand for all Kurdish experience. UNESCO’s assessment concerns a particular property and its Hawrami communities; it is not a description of every Kurdish town, livelihood, or household. [s3](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1647/)",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-dress",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dress records differences within the community",
        "content": "Historical research on Kurdish clothing distinguishes ensembles associated with Mahabad, Sanandaj, Kermanshah, and the Kurdish communities around Quchan in Khorasan. It describes differences in garments, head coverings, and combinations of layers, alongside variation by age, social position, season, and occasion. [s4](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/clothing-xvi/)\n\nThe study is valuable precisely because it resists the idea of one standard costume. Its observations belong to the period of its underlying research, rather than establishing what everyone wears today. Read together, regional dress, literary patronage, and the Hawraman landscape reveal cultural continuity through particular practices, without turning Kurdish people into a timeless or uniform image. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/kurdish-written-literature/?generate_pdf=1) [s4](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/clothing-xvi/)",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
